WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.190
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.190 --> 00:00:06.630
If you had the chance to live on an active
volcano, floating in the ocean, in the middle

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.469
of nowhere, that erupts on a regular basis
and where the air is so toxic that you are

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:15.740
required to wear a gas mask, would you take
up this wonderful opportunity?

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.740
No?

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.090
What’s wrong with you?

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.980
The two-and-a-half-thousand happy residents
of Miyake Island have nothing but good words

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.300
to say about their cosy-little home.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.789
Look, they are practically welcoming you with
open arms…

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:35.390
Known to the Japanese as Miyake-jima, this
island volcano last erupted in June 2000 after

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.070
a 17-year lull.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:42.680
The island’s resident volcano is called
Mount Oyama, and she is one hell of a violent

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.680
madam.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.150
In the year 2000, not only did she erupt with
full force, sending ash plumes 10 miles high

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:53.790
into the air, but the eruption also caused
over seventeen-and-a-half-thousand subsequent

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.260
earthquakes, which lasted for a month.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.480
These earthquakes caused major cracks in the
Earth’s crust, beneath the island.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.510
Several craters also collapsed causing major
rifts in the ground.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:10.650
Ever since these huge tears in the volcano
have constantly released highly toxic sulphur

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.480
dioxide into the air.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.840
This caused a whole twenty per cent of the
island to become uninhabitable immediately

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.150
afterwards.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.829
The islanders have always had issues with
sulphur dioxide being released from the volcano

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:29.360
and gas masks are not a new thing for the
island, but this drastic event made matters

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.090
a whole lot worse.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.240
So the Japanese government quickly took action
and evacuated the entirety of the island’s

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.240
residents.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.649
They banned them from their homes for the
next five years.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.479
For this entire time the island was declared
off-limits to anyone and everyone, turning

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:48.479
it into a ghost town, resembling Chernobyl.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:55.299
Mount Oyama continued releasing over 20,000
tons of sulphur dioxide every day.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:02:01.329
Over a few years the poisonous gases started
to calm down slightly and eventually in 2005

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:06.930
the evacuation order was lifted and people
were finally permitted to go back to their

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:07.930
homes.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.000
However, the constant release of toxic gases
from the volcano hadn’t stopped and it still

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.910
hasn’t to this day.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:20.030
So surely people didn’t return to their
homes, knowing they would return to a level

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.750
of constant pollution that could drastically
shorten their lives.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.830
Nope, 2,800 residents chose to return to the
island, the few that didn’t remained in

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:34.050
their Tokyo homes that were given to them
when they were evacuated.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:40.630
But most did return, I guess the draw of what
was once your home never goes away.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:46.410
But there was a catch, residents returning
to the island are required to carry gas masks

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:52.850
with them at all times, they are not required
to always wear them, although it is advised,

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:56.820
since the pollution levels all over the island
are still highly toxic.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.690
If the sulphur dioxide levels suddenly rise
to fatal levels an alarm is sounded across

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.390
the entire island and all residents must immediately
equip their masks.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:11.170
On top of all this, a whole two-thirds of
the island are still completely off-limits

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.110
today, due to extremely high toxicity levels.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.670
The government conducts regular health checks
on all the island’s residents to ensure

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:22.820
they’re tickety-boo, despite the omnipresent
cloud of murderous gas.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.500
What’s even more crazy than wanting to live
in this environment is that the island now

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.940
has a thriving tourist trade.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.850
Japanese mainlanders and occasionally foreigners,
flock to the island in their thousands every

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.030
year, to take tours of abandoned houses and
more.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:43.310
A perennial favourite is a visit to a school
that was half destroyed by lava.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.140
Of course disposable gas masks are sold to
tourists at local stores all over the island.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.382
I guess it’s quite the adrenaline rush to
explore somewhere that could kill you if you

00:03:52.382 --> 00:03:55.330
breathed in the air for a little too long.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:59.640
So now you’ve learnt of all the wonders
that this killer island has to offer, have

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.120
you reconsidered?

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.070
Would you live on Miyake-Jima island?

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:08.920
If for some bizarre reason your answer is
yes then I’ll leave you with this thought,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:26.940
due to the sulphur dioxide present in the
air, the entire island smells of farts, 24/7.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:38.570
Thanks for watching.

